1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: And the base can look grey, simply because less light makes it through.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: The same kind of scattering makes haze look whitish, and puts a bright white glow around the Sun.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: Scattered skylight has another property that our eyes can't see: it's polarised.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: Polarised meaning what, exactly?

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: Light waves can vibrate in any direction across their path. In polarised light, one direction wins out.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: And skylight is most strongly polarised in a band 90 degrees away from the Sun.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: There, in a clear sky, the polarisation can reach about eighty percent.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: Polarising sunglasses can make that part of the sky look a deeper blue.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: Rayleigh's first papers on the sky, in eighteen seventy-one, were even titled: On the light from the sky, its polarization and colour.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERB: So the polarisation was part of the puzzle from the very start.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: And many insects can sense this pattern and use it as a compass.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: The idea goes back to Karl von Frisch's studies of honeybees.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: From the pattern alone, an animal can work out where the Sun is, even when it's out of sight.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: A compass painted across the whole sky.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: It has even been suggested that Viking sailors did something similar, with a crystal called a sunstone.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: As the day ends, the same rule paints the sky in different colours.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: When the Sun is high, its light takes the shortest route through the air.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: Squeeze all the air above you to sea-level density, and it would be only about 8 kilometres deep.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: Only eight kilometres? That's a thin blanket.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: But near the horizon, sunlight slants through far more air: about 38 times as much.
